1
00:00:11,103 --> 00:00:36,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're physically secluded from from company, from others, a practice of of some reflexive questioning can really take take root.

2
00:00:36,754 --> 00:00:47,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's a... there's a difference between how... how we feel when we are alone and how we feel in the proximity of other people.

3
00:00:47,619 --> 00:00:51,021
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a fundamental existential difference.

4
00:00:52,722 --> 00:01:01,346
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that type of solitude is necessary for, eh... for any kind of meditation that leads to understanding.

5
00:01:03,647 --> 00:01:05,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Any kind of meditation that it's not about

6
00:01:06,209 --> 00:01:10,772
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about shutting out the world of senses, but it's about fully understanding it.

7
00:01:15,895 --> 00:01:30,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reflexive questioning, it's more of an attitude towards your own mind, than it is an actual questioning and looking for answers.

8
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:50,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once we're alone, quiet, calm, you can just ask yourself, what is the mind thinking right now?

9
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:56,419
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the point is, don't give the answers right away.

10
00:01:57,419 --> 00:02:00,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't jump at answering and feel this or it's thinking this.

11
00:02:02,229 --> 00:02:13,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Just use the question as, eh... as directing your own attention towards the context in which your mind is.

12
00:02:13,835 --> 00:02:21,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Not towards the particular... particular thoughts you might be thinking.

13
00:02:21,218 --> 00:02:22,659
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, let that context endure.

14
00:02:22,859 --> 00:02:26,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Let that question... let that direction revealed by question endure.

15
00:02:29,782 --> 00:02:30,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not really

16
00:02:33,471 --> 00:02:41,337
[SPEAKER_00]: About the content of the answer, it's about discerning the context behind it.

17
00:02:43,419 --> 00:02:56,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the mind thinking right now, while this talk is being listened to, while you're seated, while you're alone?

18
00:02:57,769 --> 00:02:59,030
[SPEAKER_00]: What is in the background of that?

19
00:02:59,611 --> 00:03:00,211
[SPEAKER_00]: What thoughts?

20
00:03:00,491 --> 00:03:01,072
[SPEAKER_00]: What moods?

21
00:03:03,758 --> 00:03:20,368
[SPEAKER_00]: And learn how to see them in that background, on the level of that background, without overdoing their analysis or rationalizing.

22
00:03:21,669 --> 00:03:29,534
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to see them as well, because by not seeing them and not rationalizing them, you'll be mystifying it.

23
00:03:30,494 --> 00:03:31,415
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want that either.

24
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,284
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see it on its own terms.

25
00:03:35,304 --> 00:03:42,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And its own terms are terms of the background and a degree of ambiguity and less of the particular clarity.

26
00:03:42,994 --> 00:03:49,663
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just the nature of those fundamental thoughts that determine your mood, that then determine your particular actions and so on.

27
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:03,110
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what thoughts are present right now?

28
00:04:26,795 --> 00:04:27,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You let that endure.

29
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:29,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Did the mind start slipping away?

30
00:04:31,660 --> 00:04:35,461
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind starts slipping away, you find something more defined to think about.

31
00:04:35,961 --> 00:04:39,682
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, what are my thoughts right now?

32
00:04:39,702 --> 00:04:42,623
[SPEAKER_00]: How am I feeling right now, in regard to those thoughts?

33
00:04:43,023 --> 00:04:44,704
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my fundamental mood?

34
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:47,545
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my experience as a whole?

35
00:04:50,406 --> 00:04:54,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I feeling it pleasantly or unpleasantly?

36
00:04:59,372 --> 00:05:03,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I aware of my situation right now that I'm in?

37
00:05:05,616 --> 00:05:19,325
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever thoughts might be coming up, for example, you might be concerned about not being able to maintain sufficient solitude.

38
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:27,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you might be concerned that you might lose the track of your mind once you get out of the solitude.

39
00:05:30,514 --> 00:05:32,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you start looking into that further.

40
00:05:32,417 --> 00:05:37,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Why are you concerned with the loss of this particular composure?

41
00:05:39,688 --> 00:05:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: What is concerning about that?

42
00:05:54,831 --> 00:05:56,332
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no right or wrong answer.

43
00:05:56,812 --> 00:06:01,354
[SPEAKER_00]: You just want to see what's behind it, what's behind that concern, hence the question.

44
00:06:04,155 --> 00:06:14,579
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, you might have to be holding yourself back a bit in terms of trying to formulate the answer and learning how to see behind the question.

45
00:06:14,899 --> 00:06:20,621
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order to see behind the question, questioning is necessary for that.

46
00:06:21,702 --> 00:06:24,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you need to know what question should be asked.

47
00:06:28,132 --> 00:06:34,618
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case, the question is, why would I be affected by the loss of composure in the mind?

48
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:48,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So far, based on my experience throughout this life, this mind of mine, do I have any say in its changing?

49
00:06:50,892 --> 00:06:54,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have any say in its deciding this or that?

50
00:06:56,509 --> 00:06:59,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize there is only a relative amount of say.

51
00:07:01,413 --> 00:07:08,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Overall, you are kind of subjected to the weathers of your mind, to the moods, basically.

52
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:15,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you subjected to something that you are not in control of?

53
00:07:16,985 --> 00:07:18,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, don't answer it.

54
00:07:19,907 --> 00:07:21,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask the question and let it endure.

55
00:07:23,570 --> 00:07:25,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it endure for a whole day, if you need to.

56
00:07:26,243 --> 00:07:33,866
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point is allowing things to endure on the level of these moods and discernments of nature of things.

57
00:07:33,946 --> 00:07:36,106
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not concerned with a particular content.

58
00:07:38,427 --> 00:07:46,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you subjected to something that you are not in control of, that you are not the subject of?

59
00:07:48,690 --> 00:07:50,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind has its own moods.

60
00:07:51,730 --> 00:07:56,073
[SPEAKER_00]: arises, ceases, either way, it endures one way or the other.

61
00:07:57,094 --> 00:08:04,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you are the one that's seemingly being affected by it, by the unpleasant mood, by the pleasant mood.

62
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:15,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be affected now with a concern of losing solitude or or not being able to to maintain the composure when you go back into the world.

63
00:08:16,688 --> 00:08:17,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

64
00:08:17,649 --> 00:08:19,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is the loss of composure a worry?

65
00:08:24,023 --> 00:08:25,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might do something unwholesome?

66
00:08:31,066 --> 00:08:33,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is doing something unwholesome troubling you?

67
00:08:39,769 --> 00:08:41,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it will fill you with remorse or similar?

68
00:08:42,350 --> 00:08:44,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is remorse a problem?

69
00:08:47,252 --> 00:08:48,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's unpleasant.

70
00:08:49,053 --> 00:08:52,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And, above all, because it's not in your control.

71
00:08:54,590 --> 00:09:03,836
[SPEAKER_00]: If we were in control, we would never be remorseful, or sad, or unhappy, or disappointed.

72
00:09:04,997 --> 00:09:05,577
[SPEAKER_00]: We just wouldn't.

73
00:09:07,178 --> 00:09:17,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, the problem here is that you're subjected to these things of your own mind, of your own doing, in a way, but you are not in control and that's...

74
00:09:17,985 --> 00:09:21,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you are subjected to the disagreeable, that is your suffering then.

75
00:09:28,643 --> 00:09:39,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, bottom line is then, you don't want to lose composure, you're afraid of compromising solitude, that might compromise composure, that might compromise the currently agreeable state of mind.

76
00:09:43,072 --> 00:09:50,096
[SPEAKER_00]: But if currently agreeable state of mind were yours to begin with, it would not change.

77
00:09:50,136 --> 00:09:52,898
[SPEAKER_00]: It would stay for as long as you want it to stay.

78
00:09:55,539 --> 00:09:56,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And you always want to stay.

79
00:09:59,032 --> 00:10:01,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody wants disagreeable.

80
00:10:01,633 --> 00:10:05,855
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if people want disagreeable things, it's because they find that agreeable.

81
00:10:06,555 --> 00:10:14,539
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just the most fundamental motivation, pleasure, be it happiness or peace.

82
00:10:14,779 --> 00:10:16,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, point is agreeable.

83
00:10:20,767 --> 00:10:36,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, all these doubts you might have, fears, worries about this, about that, about what if I lose my mindfulness and then I say things that then I regret to, or I do things, or what if this piece doesn't last as long as I want.

84
00:10:36,893 --> 00:10:48,337
[SPEAKER_00]: All of these concerns, all these particular proliferations, possibilities of these concerns, are all rooted in one one thing that is here, right now, with you.

85
00:10:54,016 --> 00:11:08,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that is then the problem, in all these particular things you might be worrying about, how can you uproot it?

86
00:11:10,408 --> 00:11:16,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, what would be the better way of going about it?

87
00:11:18,914 --> 00:11:19,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if

88
00:11:21,401 --> 00:11:26,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing solitude worries you, because you might cause suffering.

89
00:11:26,765 --> 00:11:31,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Losing your mindfulness worries you, because you might result in suffering.

90
00:11:31,549 --> 00:11:38,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing things, saying things, thinking things, worry you, because you might cause suffering.

91
00:11:39,355 --> 00:11:48,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is a better way to go, in order to try to understand this and overcome that issue?

92
00:11:49,683 --> 00:11:50,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it be

93
00:11:52,412 --> 00:11:53,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it be trying to protect your solitude?

94
00:11:54,593 --> 00:12:01,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it be trying forcefully or with full determination to never lose your mindfulness?

95
00:12:02,295 --> 00:12:07,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Would it be never saying or doing anything that might upset anyone?

96
00:12:10,958 --> 00:12:20,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, would it be freeing yourself from possibility to suffer in regard to loss of control?

97
00:12:22,712 --> 00:12:51,703
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to anything, which is more likely to pertain to the actual freedom from suffering, trying to manage things particularly, trying to protect your environment, trying to deal with it, trying to deal with remorse, with things that come as a result of you losing your control or losing the sight of it, or understanding

98
00:12:53,705 --> 00:13:04,967
[SPEAKER_00]: What's in common to all these things, which is you not wanting pain of any kind.

99
00:13:06,287 --> 00:13:17,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the question should be asked is, am I right in gratuitously assuming the attitude of not wanting this pleasure?

100
00:13:21,210 --> 00:13:22,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I right in taking it for granted?

101
00:13:24,441 --> 00:13:25,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I right in holding it?

102
00:13:26,543 --> 00:13:27,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I right in maintaining it?

103
00:13:30,386 --> 00:13:31,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have a right to do so?

104
00:13:34,450 --> 00:13:46,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Because attitude towards the possibility of a disagreeable feeling does imply a certain entitlement from your point of view.

105
00:13:48,502 --> 00:13:50,704
[SPEAKER_00]: You think you have a right to have a say.

106
00:13:55,127 --> 00:13:56,087
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that really the case?

107
00:14:01,448 --> 00:14:06,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you had a say in the first place, you wouldn't need to be wanting anything.

108
00:14:06,369 --> 00:14:09,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Things would just obey your command, your control, your will.

109
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:11,630
[SPEAKER_00]: But they don't.

110
00:14:13,271 --> 00:14:19,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you thinking you have a say in the first place, it's actually secondary to the things themselves.

111
00:14:19,632 --> 00:14:23,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you thinking you have a say, kind of puts you first.

112
00:14:25,843 --> 00:14:29,125
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that very discrepancy that is the root of all the suffering.

113
00:14:30,646 --> 00:14:31,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely nothing else.

114
00:14:34,809 --> 00:14:52,160
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said... well, he never said that suffering is caused because of this, or because of that, or because somebody done something disagreeable, or we didn't get enough of agreeable, or... All these things are manifestations of suffering, but suffering, in itself,

115
00:14:54,553 --> 00:14:57,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's entirely, completely rooted in craving.

116
00:14:59,476 --> 00:15:06,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, presence of craving, presence of that discrepancy that I'm talking about, means presence of suffering.

117
00:15:10,544 --> 00:15:22,032
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if then, the discrepancy is removed, would you be able to suffer, even if things do appear as disagreeable?

118
00:15:30,387 --> 00:15:32,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the important distinction here.

119
00:15:33,408 --> 00:15:37,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody who read the suttas would say, yes, craving is suffering.

120
00:15:38,130 --> 00:15:52,516
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's actually... the point is discerning the tacit attitude that everybody has in still assuming that say in things.

121
00:15:56,657 --> 00:15:58,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And by that, I mean say towards the

122
00:15:59,465 --> 00:16:03,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Like present enduring feeling, you have a say in it, by wanting it or not wanting it.

123
00:16:04,669 --> 00:16:18,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, practically, you know the feeling doesn't obey your will whatsoever, but that doesn't stop you from still maintaining that assumption that it should.

124
00:16:18,899 --> 00:16:28,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And that assumption is maintained through carelessly, eh... holding on an attitude of

125
00:16:28,685 --> 00:16:29,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wanting pain.

126
00:16:32,748 --> 00:16:34,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Wanting to get rid of the arisen pain.

127
00:16:36,570 --> 00:16:39,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And the attitude of wanting to prolong the arisen pleasure.

128
00:16:42,655 --> 00:16:44,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's fine to not want pain.

129
00:16:45,336 --> 00:16:56,484
[SPEAKER_00]: But what it's not fine is that within the present enduring feeling, whatever that feeling is, to maintain that stance that creates the discrepancy.

130
00:16:57,758 --> 00:16:59,298
[SPEAKER_00]: In pleasure, you want more.

131
00:17:00,419 --> 00:17:01,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the discrepancy.

132
00:17:01,739 --> 00:17:02,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence the suffering.

133
00:17:03,519 --> 00:17:05,360
[SPEAKER_00]: In pain, you want less.

134
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:06,700
[SPEAKER_00]: You want the absence of it.

135
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:07,640
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's present.

136
00:17:08,661 --> 00:17:11,801
[SPEAKER_00]: But you... your attitude is attitude of wanting it to go.

137
00:17:13,582 --> 00:17:14,922
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another discrepancy.

138
00:17:18,563 --> 00:17:24,124
[SPEAKER_00]: And practically, those are the two feelings one can be practicing in regard to.

139
00:17:24,304 --> 00:17:26,185
[SPEAKER_00]: There is obviously the neutral feeling as well.

140
00:17:29,311 --> 00:17:41,823
[SPEAKER_00]: It's slightly harder to discern, but even within that, you can, because when it's neither pleasant nor unpleasant, what the mind wants is distraction from that neutral presence.

141
00:17:42,383 --> 00:17:45,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the discrepancy, hence the suffering.

142
00:17:46,127 --> 00:17:54,034
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said that neutral feeling, if it's not known, it's felt unpleasantly.

143
00:17:55,174 --> 00:17:59,618
[SPEAKER_00]: And you create a discrepancy when you don't know that you couldn't have created it in the first place.

144
00:18:05,422 --> 00:18:07,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is how every meditation should go.

145
00:18:08,185 --> 00:18:20,074
[SPEAKER_00]: For yourself, secluded, contemplating rightly, asking the right questions, and then letting the direction revealed by those questions endure.

146
00:18:20,655 --> 00:18:24,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't jump into answers, but don't over-mystify and turn it into a brain fog.

147
00:18:26,342 --> 00:18:28,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You're after clarity, clarity of understanding.

148
00:18:29,523 --> 00:18:37,365
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through thinking about nature of suffering, you get closer to understanding it.

149
00:18:37,845 --> 00:18:43,527
[SPEAKER_00]: You get closer in seeing that your concern with particular things that bother you is not your suffering.

150
00:18:44,547 --> 00:18:54,930
[SPEAKER_00]: The suffering is the presence of the discrepancy that you created in regard to things that bother you or in regard to things that don't bother you.

151
00:18:59,387 --> 00:19:09,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Because can you remain indifferent in regard to pleasure, in regard to happiness, in regard to possibility of pleasure and happiness?

152
00:19:10,995 --> 00:19:15,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Will your mind not remain, not become elated towards it?

153
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:19,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Will your mind not want more of it?

154
00:19:22,844 --> 00:19:26,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Will your mind try not to make it stay forever?

155
00:19:29,913 --> 00:19:32,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you remain imperturb... imperturbable?

156
00:19:33,295 --> 00:19:38,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you remain imperturbable in regard to pleasure?

157
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:44,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Or can you remain imperturbable in regard to pain?

158
00:19:45,942 --> 00:19:48,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you remain mindful and composed in regard to neutral feeling?

159
00:19:49,124 --> 00:19:55,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Or will your mind turn and find something to do that will distract itself from itself?

160
00:19:59,678 --> 00:20:02,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the answer is yes, then you wonder, so what do I do?

161
00:20:03,121 --> 00:20:17,314
[SPEAKER_00]: What you do is keep bringing back into the mind these very things, because it's... it's the very ignoring of these truths and characteristics of... of... of the... behind the motivations of your actions.

162
00:20:18,776 --> 00:20:19,917
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you maintain them.

163
00:20:26,185 --> 00:20:27,866
[SPEAKER_00]: And you ignore them, when you not see them.

164
00:20:28,227 --> 00:20:29,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And you not see them, because you ignore them.

165
00:20:30,909 --> 00:20:32,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a vicious circle.

166
00:20:33,591 --> 00:20:47,061
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start looking at least in that direction, through asking yourself the right questions, for the sake of revealing the direction behind it, then you're ignoring it less and less.

167
00:20:47,161 --> 00:20:50,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And eventually, you will become unable to ignore it.

168
00:20:50,684 --> 00:20:50,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

169
00:20:51,064 --> 00:20:52,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the universal truth.

170
00:20:52,865 --> 00:20:53,346
[SPEAKER_00]: The dhamma.

171
00:20:53,506 --> 00:20:54,707
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said himself.

172
00:20:56,122 --> 00:20:57,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a creation of a theory or something.

173
00:20:57,923 --> 00:21:01,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just revealing the nature of things that are there.

174
00:21:01,325 --> 00:21:03,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you want them or not makes no difference.

175
00:21:04,187 --> 00:21:24,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And through understanding the right nature, for which you need to have that right instruction, hence the right questions, then you will be unable to maintain all the discrepancy, all the sense of... sense of control and mastery of things, all the gratuitous notions of I have a say or... or I at least imply.

176
00:21:26,081 --> 00:21:29,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Having a say through my attitude, in regard to what I'm feeling right now.

177
00:21:31,762 --> 00:21:41,724
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, whether it's pleasure, pain, neutral feeling, there will be no discrepancy maintained by the mind in regard to it.

178
00:21:42,784 --> 00:21:45,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And if there is no discrepancy, there's no suffering.

179
00:21:46,425 --> 00:21:53,826
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is in the scene, there is just a scene, in the herd, there's just a herd, and so on for all the senses, as that Bhagya Sutta says.

180
00:22:00,087 --> 00:22:01,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is my mind thinking right now?

181
00:22:01,748 --> 00:22:02,689
[SPEAKER_00]: You can ask yourself that.

182
00:22:03,390 --> 00:22:08,895
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer is... if the answer is nothing specifically, that's fine.

183
00:22:08,935 --> 00:22:10,456
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reason experience as a whole.

184
00:22:11,757 --> 00:22:17,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are the background thoughts present in regard to your non-specific thinking right here, right now?

185
00:22:20,745 --> 00:22:21,926
[SPEAKER_00]: What's at the back of your mind?

186
00:22:22,266 --> 00:22:24,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't try to make it to the front.

187
00:22:24,788 --> 00:22:26,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Just see it at the back, but see it.

188
00:22:26,790 --> 00:22:27,671
[SPEAKER_00]: What's in that distance?

189
00:22:29,123 --> 00:22:30,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that ambiguous fog.

190
00:22:31,364 --> 00:22:31,725
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

191
00:22:32,765 --> 00:22:33,486
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it thinking?

192
00:22:34,827 --> 00:22:36,588
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be a general, ambiguous thought.

193
00:22:37,789 --> 00:22:41,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't over-define it again, but don't ignore it either.

194
00:22:42,792 --> 00:22:45,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't know how to relate to it, see how it feels.

195
00:22:45,954 --> 00:22:47,475
[SPEAKER_00]: How are you feeling in regard to that thought?

196
00:22:48,756 --> 00:22:49,876
[SPEAKER_00]: At the background of your mind.

197
00:22:51,737 --> 00:22:57,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, at the background of your mind, there is a certain discomfort because of the, eh...

198
00:22:59,645 --> 00:23:06,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Implicit pressure of the duties that I have to do in couple of hours time.

199
00:23:06,570 --> 00:23:08,651
[SPEAKER_00]: To go to work, I have to do this, I have to do that.

200
00:23:10,813 --> 00:23:11,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

201
00:23:11,293 --> 00:23:11,953
[SPEAKER_00]: The Bhagavad-gītā.

202
00:23:11,973 --> 00:23:14,495
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not thinking about it, but it's kind of there.

203
00:23:16,036 --> 00:23:22,060
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see that that... that's being... that state of mind, that mood is being felt.

204
00:23:24,922 --> 00:23:28,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's perceived in that ambiguous domain.

205
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:45,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you want to do is, without trying to over-define it, you want to learn how to allow that, eh... discernment, that recognition to endure.

206
00:23:46,508 --> 00:23:55,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognized that there is a certain amount of discomfort in the background of your mind, because of something you don't want to do, but you have to do.

207
00:23:56,916 --> 00:23:57,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's fine.

208
00:23:58,377 --> 00:24:01,519
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... through not seeing it,

209
00:24:04,048 --> 00:24:16,916
[SPEAKER_00]: You immediately default, or rather, by not seeing it, the starting point, the first attitude towards something that's not seen, is not wanting it, if it's disagreeable, and wanting it, if it's agreeable.

210
00:24:17,937 --> 00:24:19,518
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the discrepancy we talked about.

211
00:24:21,799 --> 00:24:27,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you're discerning that, in the background of your mind, there is... so, you don't have to, like, overly psychologize it.

212
00:24:27,843 --> 00:24:31,045
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to rationalize it and explain to yourself how duties must be performed.

213
00:24:31,961 --> 00:24:34,023
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that is questioned here.

214
00:24:34,323 --> 00:24:35,664
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that is a problem here.

215
00:24:37,045 --> 00:24:38,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we all must perform duties.

216
00:24:42,529 --> 00:24:43,589
[SPEAKER_00]: We all have things to do.

217
00:24:45,751 --> 00:25:01,562
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, your attitude in regard to it, on the level of that background, on the level at the back of your mind, where you're not used to thinking about things, you only think about things that are in front of you,

218
00:25:02,118 --> 00:25:15,209
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to think about something, you need to bring it to the front, which means you're then not seeing these little ambiguous general backgrounds and moods that are actually more fundamental than your particular thoughts.

219
00:25:15,769 --> 00:25:22,395
[SPEAKER_00]: They are fuel... they are sort of directing you, like... like the wind gently blowing into the sails.

220
00:25:23,813 --> 00:25:32,159
[SPEAKER_00]: It might not be a very discernible or strong wind, but the point is, there is a constant exertion of its pressure against the sail.

221
00:25:32,199 --> 00:25:34,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will be moving the way it directs you.

222
00:25:38,042 --> 00:25:49,170
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the Buddha said, the fundamental reason for being directed by this discrepancy, by the greed aversion, is ignoring it, ignorance of it.

223
00:25:50,531 --> 00:25:52,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... not having it seen,

224
00:25:53,175 --> 00:26:05,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Not seeing it, not having it seen, not discerning it, not allowing it to endure in that and develop clarity in regard to it.

225
00:26:06,018 --> 00:26:11,019
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I say clarity, I don't mean to rationalize it and to collect the data about it.

226
00:26:11,299 --> 00:26:12,399
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all secondary to it.

227
00:26:12,860 --> 00:26:16,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarity means whenever you look at the background, you will see it.

228
00:26:16,961 --> 00:26:20,081
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to spend half an hour contemplating, preparing yourself.

229
00:26:20,141 --> 00:26:21,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it will.

230
00:26:21,302 --> 00:26:22,522
[SPEAKER_00]: You will see these things.

231
00:26:23,317 --> 00:26:24,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And how you develop that?

232
00:26:24,538 --> 00:26:34,285
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop it through trying to see it, through clarifying the meaning, the context, by clarifying the nature of it.

233
00:26:40,749 --> 00:26:50,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if the mind is more composed, and you have that clarity in regard to the present, enduring feeling of discomfort,

234
00:26:53,563 --> 00:26:59,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of the pressure of duties and things you don't want to do in two hours' time.

235
00:27:03,029 --> 00:27:04,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So, did you create that feeling?

236
00:27:05,771 --> 00:27:08,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Did it come from you, that feeling of discomfort?

237
00:27:14,136 --> 00:27:14,297
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

238
00:27:18,499 --> 00:27:19,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you found it there.

239
00:27:23,946 --> 00:27:30,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Through looking at the background perception, so to speak, you discerned there is discomfort there.

240
00:27:31,149 --> 00:27:33,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you found it there, which means it was already there on its own.

241
00:27:36,191 --> 00:27:40,452
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something was already there on its own, you haven't created it.

242
00:27:42,713 --> 00:27:47,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't even conceive where it came from, as in, oh, it's because of this and this and this.

243
00:27:49,142 --> 00:27:50,082
[SPEAKER_00]: You just found it there.

244
00:27:50,422 --> 00:28:04,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you might rationalize that it's because of this, this, this, but all that is secondary to the independent presence, present enduring of that feeling of discomfort in regard to the duties that you have to perform in two hours time.

245
00:28:07,606 --> 00:28:14,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something is just found there, independent of you, its origins pretty much inconceivable to you.

246
00:28:19,713 --> 00:28:24,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you justified in having the attitude of wanting it to go away?

247
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:42,592
[SPEAKER_00]: So, out of the two of you, you and that disagreeable discomfort, who is more right, basically, to be there?

248
00:28:43,072 --> 00:28:44,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Whose attitude is more correct?

249
00:28:45,823 --> 00:29:01,672
[SPEAKER_00]: The attitude of that discomfort of a feeling, that's just discomfort, that doesn't want to do some work, or is it you feeling entitled to not want it?

250
00:29:06,274 --> 00:29:09,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Who out of two of you is not minding their own business?

251
00:29:11,417 --> 00:29:12,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Clearly, that feeling is.

252
00:29:14,812 --> 00:29:15,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's unaware of you.

253
00:29:19,376 --> 00:29:21,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it's you who is not minding your own business.

254
00:29:22,378 --> 00:29:24,280
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you who interferes with disagreeable.

255
00:29:24,580 --> 00:29:26,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you who interferes with agreeable.

256
00:29:26,782 --> 00:29:28,383
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you who interferes with neutral.

257
00:29:29,784 --> 00:29:30,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

258
00:29:32,386 --> 00:29:35,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you haven't fully realized that you can't interfere in the first place.

259
00:29:37,650 --> 00:29:40,833
[SPEAKER_00]: That the interference cannot change the nature of things.

260
00:29:41,854 --> 00:29:44,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of interference means presence of suffering.

261
00:29:45,592 --> 00:29:46,853
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of things remains the same.

262
00:29:48,094 --> 00:29:51,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of interference means absence of suffering.

263
00:29:51,376 --> 00:29:53,217
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of things remains the same.

264
00:29:53,798 --> 00:29:55,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Five aggregates are five aggregates.

265
00:29:55,419 --> 00:29:56,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Puputujana or foranarhana.

266
00:29:56,900 --> 00:29:57,581
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

267
00:29:57,841 --> 00:29:58,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same.

268
00:29:59,022 --> 00:30:02,064
[SPEAKER_00]: The difference is the assumption in regard to it.

269
00:30:10,370 --> 00:30:12,291
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all a person needs to do, really.

270
00:30:13,934 --> 00:30:19,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Great revelation or secret revealed and they will then result in freedom of the mind.

271
00:30:19,821 --> 00:30:19,941
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

272
00:30:19,961 --> 00:30:32,274
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just learning how to stop ignoring the fundamental characteristics, the nature of things, of your feelings, your perceptions, your moods, your intentions.

273
00:30:32,374 --> 00:30:32,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

274
00:30:34,026 --> 00:30:43,028
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no like a perfect set of perceptions and feelings you you you develop bit by bit and then it kind of culminates in this great great manifestation.

275
00:30:43,148 --> 00:30:48,389
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's actually undoing your perception, because everybody starts as a puthujjana.

276
00:30:49,810 --> 00:30:53,831
[SPEAKER_00]: So, puthujjana cannot, on the basis of puthujjana, build his arahantship.

277
00:30:55,751 --> 00:30:59,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Puthujjana can become an arahant by undoing his state of puthujjana.

278
00:31:03,257 --> 00:31:11,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's... it's, eh... individually, to be known by the wise, internally, by undoing yourself, basically.

279
00:31:12,002 --> 00:31:13,683
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why nobody can do that for you.

280
00:31:17,244 --> 00:31:29,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And you undo things, you undo these gratuitous attitudes and notions that result directly in your suffering, through understanding them, through discerning them.

281
00:31:30,231 --> 00:31:30,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

282
00:31:30,552 --> 00:31:30,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

283
00:31:31,674 --> 00:31:36,755
[SPEAKER_00]: The basis for their existence is being ignored.

284
00:31:36,775 --> 00:31:38,676
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what a vijja is.

285
00:31:38,716 --> 00:31:43,377
[SPEAKER_00]: And you ignore something for various reasons, but the point is, it's being ignored.

286
00:31:44,177 --> 00:31:56,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You ignore something through giving in gratuitously, through greed, aversion, delusion, which are all results, secondary results, to that discrepancy that your attitude fundamentally creates.

287
00:31:58,789 --> 00:32:04,510
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, obviously, through taking for granted the results of that discrepancy, you carry on the discrepancy.

288
00:32:05,130 --> 00:32:08,731
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why... that's why the sensory strain needs to come first.

289
00:32:08,751 --> 00:32:15,152
[SPEAKER_00]: That we have to stop engaging with greed, aversion, delusion, in order then to discern the attitude behind it.

290
00:32:22,554 --> 00:32:27,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can ask yourself now, has your mood changed?

291
00:32:28,408 --> 00:32:29,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Has your mood changed?

292
00:32:29,829 --> 00:32:31,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever is being felt before?

293
00:32:32,652 --> 00:32:34,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it still the same thing enduring there?

294
00:32:36,135 --> 00:32:38,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you might say, oh, it's slightly different, but overall it's the same.

295
00:32:38,637 --> 00:32:39,618
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the same thing.

296
00:32:42,480 --> 00:32:46,623
[SPEAKER_00]: And we gave the example of, say, that discomfort because of something you have to do after this.

297
00:32:50,166 --> 00:32:50,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it bother you?

298
00:32:52,749 --> 00:32:56,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, discomfort, it's discomfortable in itself, but does it bother you?

299
00:32:58,658 --> 00:33:06,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it bother you now, when you have looked at it, when you've seen it, when you've discerned it in the background?

300
00:33:06,740 --> 00:33:09,160
[SPEAKER_00]: It might bother you, but does it bother you as much as before?

301
00:33:10,240 --> 00:33:21,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel that pressure exercised over you to the same extent as it was when you were not aware, or rather turning a blind eye towards that which was pressuring you?

302
00:33:24,583 --> 00:33:25,384
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it cannot.

303
00:33:25,404 --> 00:33:26,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot exercise the same pressure.

304
00:33:26,824 --> 00:33:27,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the most

305
00:33:28,292 --> 00:33:39,665
[SPEAKER_00]: A direct release of the pressure, a direct, immediate, instantaneous release of the burden.

306
00:33:41,267 --> 00:33:45,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you might forget about it, and it comes back, but that's fine.

307
00:33:47,114 --> 00:33:47,975
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just how it works.

308
00:33:52,060 --> 00:34:01,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Repeating the motions of discerning the nature of things rightly, they will result in you not maintaining that discrepancy in regard to discomfort or comfort.

309
00:34:04,526 --> 00:34:10,889
[SPEAKER_00]: You are gradually releasing that pressure that's being exercised over you.

310
00:34:13,491 --> 00:34:18,113
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there is no greater verification of understanding than freedom from suffering.

311
00:34:18,173 --> 00:34:19,994
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that's the only verification you need.

312
00:34:21,052 --> 00:34:26,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if the whole world comes and says that that's wrong, you know that through direct understanding of that, you are unable to suffer.

313
00:34:27,518 --> 00:34:44,733
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha meant in the suttas, that the one with the right view, even if everybody comes and tells him, no, the Buddha was completely wrong, his mind will remain unchanged, because even, like, no matter what they would say, that person, for oneself, is directly incapable of suffering.

314
00:34:45,463 --> 00:34:51,605
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like running away from it, or pretend it's not there, or having to maintain some determined meditation technique to never suffer.

315
00:34:51,645 --> 00:34:51,765
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

316
00:34:51,785 --> 00:34:55,227
[SPEAKER_00]: He fully understood that he's incapable of suffering amidst the suffering.

317
00:34:55,707 --> 00:34:57,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever he does, doesn't matter.

318
00:34:57,267 --> 00:34:59,548
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot suffer.

319
00:34:59,848 --> 00:35:08,151
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is absolutely... you cannot ask for a better reward and verification that things have been done rightly.

320
00:35:09,272 --> 00:35:10,252
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the criteria.

321
00:35:10,292 --> 00:35:15,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all this questioning should be resulting in freeing you from suffering.

322
00:35:16,317 --> 00:35:29,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Not through ignoring, not through turning a blind eye, not through trying to shut things out, but through actually discerning the nature of them on the right level, the level of that background, the level of the mood, the level of the feeling.

323
00:35:43,095 --> 00:35:52,140
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the disagreeable feeling that we gave as an example is still there, it's still enduring, you can start looking at its characteristics.

324
00:35:58,603 --> 00:36:00,204
[SPEAKER_00]: It is minding its own business, isn't it?

325
00:36:01,365 --> 00:36:03,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you think about it or not, doesn't matter.

326
00:36:04,846 --> 00:36:06,267
[SPEAKER_00]: That feeling is there, enduring.

327
00:36:08,908 --> 00:36:09,729
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are the one

328
00:36:12,367 --> 00:36:14,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Who makes oneself bother with it?

329
00:36:17,052 --> 00:36:17,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Through not seeing it.

330
00:36:20,375 --> 00:36:21,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Through not allowing it to endure.

331
00:36:24,019 --> 00:36:30,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Through giving in gratuitously to the notion of... to be get rid of.

332
00:36:45,383 --> 00:36:51,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that general feeling, whatever it was, changes, you'll find a different feeling there.

333
00:36:51,648 --> 00:36:55,031
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't see a moment in between the change.

334
00:36:55,171 --> 00:37:01,676
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just going to now look and see, oh, the disagreeable feeling is gone, which means now, automatically, something is agreeable.

335
00:37:04,499 --> 00:37:07,681
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't care about what is agreeable, you look at the feeling.

336
00:37:08,062 --> 00:37:13,186
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter, oh, it's agreeable because of this, this and this, or it's disagreeable because of this and this.

337
00:37:13,226 --> 00:37:13,907
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is,

338
00:37:14,695 --> 00:37:16,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there agreeable feeling enduring?

339
00:37:17,376 --> 00:37:19,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is there a disagreeable feeling enduring?

340
00:37:22,477 --> 00:37:22,917
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

341
00:37:22,957 --> 00:37:25,038
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no need for a further answer.

342
00:37:25,438 --> 00:37:43,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Let that question that has revealed direction of the whatever feeling is enduring right there, let it endure, because through allowing it to endure, you're allowing it... well, you're allowing yourself to see its nature, which is

343
00:37:45,070 --> 00:37:47,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Independent of you, you're not in its control.

344
00:37:47,491 --> 00:37:49,032
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot own it even if you want to.

345
00:37:49,513 --> 00:37:53,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And above all, you are entirely invisible to it.

346
00:37:55,156 --> 00:37:56,977
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for feelings.

347
00:37:57,157 --> 00:37:58,438
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for intentions.

348
00:37:58,478 --> 00:37:59,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for perceptions.

349
00:38:00,399 --> 00:38:02,560
[SPEAKER_00]: You exist because you assume your own existence.

350
00:38:04,181 --> 00:38:10,005
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why upādāna manifests power, the being.

351
00:38:12,165 --> 00:38:25,088
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five aggregates, if you're completely invisible to form, feeling, perception, intentions and consciousness, where is any room for your sense of self?

352
00:38:25,868 --> 00:38:29,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you have a thought of me, myself, that has appeared.

353
00:38:30,129 --> 00:38:32,409
[SPEAKER_00]: That's one of the aggregates, that thought.

354
00:38:34,850 --> 00:38:40,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your thought of your sense of self cannot see you,

355
00:38:41,325 --> 00:38:42,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You're invisible to it.

356
00:38:42,966 --> 00:38:47,528
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that your own sense of self is something that cannot be yours.

357
00:38:48,649 --> 00:38:49,670
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't care about you.

358
00:38:51,331 --> 00:38:52,731
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what anatta really means.

359
00:38:53,992 --> 00:38:56,673
[SPEAKER_00]: That self, which I've been taking to be mine, is not mine.

360
00:38:57,514 --> 00:38:59,795
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it becomes not-self.

361
00:39:01,036 --> 00:39:04,798
[SPEAKER_00]: But the thing that was self before and now it's not-self is still there.

362
00:39:06,267 --> 00:39:09,049
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha never said there is no self, there is not such thing.

363
00:39:09,109 --> 00:39:12,172
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he said it's the five aggregates that are taken as self.

364
00:39:13,793 --> 00:39:16,055
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the thing that is yourself is one of the aggregates.

365
00:39:16,175 --> 00:39:18,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever that aggregate is at the time, doesn't matter.

366
00:39:19,498 --> 00:39:20,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And that never was a problem.

367
00:39:22,461 --> 00:39:23,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have no say in that.

368
00:39:24,162 --> 00:39:33,350
[SPEAKER_00]: What you have say in, is in gratuitously maintaining the ownership at face value of that which has been presented as myself.

369
00:39:34,363 --> 00:39:42,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Failing to see that it's one of the aggregates, and as such, you're invisible to it, which means your very self doesn't know about you.

370
00:39:42,448 --> 00:39:43,509
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can it be yours?

371
00:39:56,016 --> 00:40:01,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Learn how to let it endure, for as long as it wants to endure, whatever it is.

372
00:40:05,875 --> 00:40:08,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to get rid of it through your meditation technique.

373
00:40:08,638 --> 00:40:09,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to ignore it.

374
00:40:10,661 --> 00:40:13,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop trying to distract yourself through sensuality.

375
00:40:14,085 --> 00:40:17,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Just recognize what's there and allow it to endure.

